 3. 🕶️ L.A. may get a socialist mayor 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 L.A. mayoral candidate Nithya Raman speaking at Axios/Noticias Telemundo Visionarios on Wednesday. Photo: Ben Camacho for Axios LOS ANGELES — Voters could make history next month by giving the two largest U.S. cities something in common: South Asian immigrant democratic socialist mayors, Axios' Russell Contreras reports. 

 Why it matters: Such a pairing would capture two forces reshaping urban America — its growing diversity and the Democratic Party's shifting ideological boundaries. New York is led by democratic socialist Mayor Zohran Mamdani.

 The challenger for L.A. mayor is Nithya Raman, an Indian-born urban planner and two-term City Council member trying to unseat Democratic Mayor Karen Bass on Nov. 3. Raman, who immigrated to the U.S. as a child, said Wednesday at the fifth annual Axios and Noticias Telemundo Visionarios event in L.A. that her family came to America seeking opportunities they didn't have in India.

 "The idea of this city welcoming an immigrant as its mayor … is just so deeply moving to me at this moment," she said. A Harvard and MIT graduate, she entered politics after working on homelessness and neighborhood organizing.

 A September Berkeley IGS/L.A. Times poll found Raman with a big lead: 39% among likely voters, with Bass at 28% and 33% undecided. Share this story . 

 
 
