 4. 🗳️ Lingo: &quot;double haters&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 More voters are unhappy with both parties in this election than in other recent ones: 21% of voters hold an unfavorable view of both parties, a Wall Street Journal poll found . That compares with 13% in the 2022 midterms and about 12% in each of the past two presidential elections.

 Why it matters: These voters — "double haters" — have often been a bellwether, breaking for the winning party. This year, they could decide the midterms. "Democrats currently lead among the double haters when voters are asked which party they will back for Congress, 55% to 27%. But about half of the voters for either party say their vote choice is just probable, not definite," the WSJ reports. 

